{# collections.articles is sorted newest-first in .eleventy.js #}
{% set latest = collections.articles | first %}

{# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Hero --
   A WebGL ray-marched nebula — a faithful port of the actual recovered
   source for 21st.dev's "liquid-shader" (the user spent a free 21st.dev
   view to unlock Component.tsx; this is Three.js/React in the original,
   ported here to vanilla WebGL on the same fullscreen-quad pattern used
   elsewhere in this file). It's a 6-step raymarch through a rotating,
   sine-warped distance field, with color accumulated along each step.
   Recolored from the original's literal teal/magenta to this site's
   brand blue/purple, and slowed substantially from the source's literal
   real-time speed per explicit feedback that an earlier hand-built
   approximation of this same effect was "amazing but too intense and
   fast." The source's `iMouse` uniform is tracked but never actually
   read in its fragment shader, so there's no cursor interaction to port
   here either. Sized and positioned to read as a big background wash
   across the whole hero — not a small glow tucked behind the photo —
   per explicit feedback that an earlier, tighter version "still mostly
   off to the side... I thought it would take up the whole space behind
   everything." A legibility mask keeps the text column clear of it
   (blended between a horizontal cut on wide screens and a vertical one
   on mobile's stacked layout — see the JS below). Runs up behind the
   nav so the two don't read as separate stacked blocks. This is a
   parallel option to the particle-wave version, not a replacement of
   it — see _hero-v7-archive.njk for that one (confirmed the best so
   far), plus _hero-v8-archive.njk (an earlier hand-built ring
   approximation), _hero-v6-archive.njk (Mesh drift WebGL shader),
   _hero-v5-archive.njk (fbm-noise domain-warp attempt),
   _hero-v4-archive.njk (CSS-only blobs + static dot grid),
   _hero-v3-archive.njk (earlier fbm-noise shader), _hero-v2-archive.njk
   (giant grayscale name) and _hero-v1-archive.njk (original card photo). -- #}
<section class="heroS">
  <canvas class="heroS__canvas" id="heroNebula" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
  <div class="heroS__inner">
    <img class="heroS__img" src="{{ home.hero.imageBw }}" alt="Jason Cypret" width="1024" height="1243">

    <div class="heroS__copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">{{ home.hero.eyebrow }}</p>
      <h1 class="heroS__h">I build the interface between <b>humans and AI.</b></h1>
      <p class="heroS__lede">
        <strong>Twenty years and 30+ products shipped</strong> as a product and design executive.
        Today I work as an AI orchestrator: I lead the team, set the direction and write a lot
        of the code myself, so product, design and engineering run as one continuous loop
        instead of three handoffs.
      </p>
      <div class="heroS__actions">
        {{ button(home.hero.ctaLabel, home.hero.ctaUrl, "primary", "md", "rounded", "", "arrow-right") }}
        <a class="inline-link inline-link--onHero" href="#work">See the work <i class="icon-arrow-down" aria-hidden="true"></i></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  /* Ray-marched nebula — ported from the real recovered source (a
     Three.js ShaderMaterial, "InteractiveNebulaShader.tsx") behind
     21st.dev's "liquid-shader". Same structure as the original: a 6-step
     raymarch through map(p) — a distance-ish field built from two
     counter-rotations plus a triple-nested sine domain warp — with color
     accumulated multiplicatively per step. The source runs iTime at full
     real-time speed with no damping at all; even after one big slowdown
     pass it still visibly drifted/morphed within a normal viewing
     session, which read as "tends to go off to the side" — tt below is
     turned down another order of magnitude on top of that, per explicit
     "I want it super slow, almost looks like a still" feedback. uZoom
     makes the structure read noticeably bigger than the source's own
     framing, so it fills the hero rather than sitting as a contained
     spot off to one side.

     Color went through two revisions. The source's literal palette
     amplifies red/magenta hardest, which first got recolored toward
     this site's brand blue/purple but kept a similar multi-hue spread —
     and in the dim, diffuse parts of the structure (which cover a lot
     more area now that uZoom is bigger) that spread reads as a muddy
     gray-purple smear, especially in light mode where it looks like a
     dirty smudge fading toward black instead of a clean glow. uNebAmp
     below instead keeps every brightness level within one blue→cyan
     hue family — no hue fight in the transition zone, so there's
     nothing left to read as muddy. The second fix is the `vivid`
     normalization: energy (how much of a pixel is "nebula") and col
     (its actual color) both came from the same raw accumulated value,
     so a pixel could cross the energy threshold while still being a
     dim, muddy version of the hue instead of a vivid one — normalizing
     col by its own max channel before blending forces every visible
     pixel to show its hue at a consistent, saturated brightness, so the
     energy curve purely controls how much shows rather than also
     controlling how washed-out it looks.

     iMouse is wired up in the source component but never actually read
     inside its fragment shader, so there's nothing to port for cursor
     interaction. Theme-aware: color blends toward the page's own
     background by how much energy landed in a pixel, so empty regions
     read as the page background in both themes, not the source's
     literal near-black resting color. */
  (function () {
    var cv = document.getElementById("heroNebula");
    if (!cv) return;
    var gl = cv.getContext("webgl", { preserveDrawingBuffer: false, alpha: false });
    if (!gl) return;

    var vs = "attribute vec2 p;void main(){gl_Position=vec4(p,0.,1.);}";
    var fs = [
      "precision highp float;",
      "uniform float t;uniform vec2 r;uniform vec2 uOff;uniform float uZoom;",
      "uniform vec3 uBase;uniform vec3 uNebBase;uniform vec3 uNebAmp;",
      "mat2 rotM(float a){float c=cos(a),s=sin(a);return mat2(c,-s,s,c);}",
      "float field(vec3 p,float tt){",
      "  p.xz*=rotM(tt*0.4);",
      "  p.xy*=rotM(tt*0.3);",
      "  vec3 q=p*2.0+tt;",
      "  return length(p+vec3(sin(tt*0.7)))*log(length(p)+1.0)",
      "       +sin(q.x+sin(q.z+sin(q.y)))*0.5-1.0;",
      "}",
      "void main(){",
      "  float tt=t*0.035;",
      // uOff is a fixed fraction (not tracked to the photo element) —
      // tracking the photo precisely turned out to fight this request:
      // the whole point now is a big wash across the hero, not a glow
      // that follows the photo around. uZoom<1 enlarges the structure
      // so it reads as filling the space rather than a contained spot.
      "  vec2 uv=(gl_FragCoord.xy/min(r.x,r.y)-uOff)*uZoom;",
      "  vec3 col=vec3(0.0);",
      "  float d=2.5;",
      "  for(int i=0;i<=5;i++){",
      "    vec3 p=vec3(0.0,0.0,5.0)+normalize(vec3(uv,-1.0))*d;",
      "    float rz=field(p,tt);",
      "    float f=clamp((rz-field(p+0.1,tt))*0.5,-0.1,1.0);",
      "    vec3 base=uNebBase+uNebAmp*f;",
      "    col=col*base+smoothstep(2.5,0.0,rz)*0.7*base;",
      "    d+=min(rz,1.0);",
      "  }",
      // Sharp energy curve — only the genuinely bright pixels count as
      // "nebula," everything dimmer collapses to background. This is
      // what gives the crisp ribbon-with-bright-core look instead of a
      // soft wash.
      "  float m=max(col.r,max(col.g,col.b));",
      "  float energy=smoothstep(0.12,0.5,m);",
      // Normalize col by its own brightness before using it as the
      // visible color — see the comment above. Without this, a pixel
      // that just barely crosses the energy threshold shows whatever
      // dim, possibly muddy value it actually accumulated, instead of a
      // clean, saturated version of its hue.
      "  vec3 vivid=clamp(col/max(m,0.001)*0.95,0.0,1.0);",
      "  vec3 shaded=mix(uBase,vivid,energy);",
      // White-hot core, matching the source's brightest highlight.
      "  float hot=smoothstep(0.55,1.1,m);",
      "  shaded=mix(shaded,vec3(1.0),hot*0.6);",
      // Legibility mask, blended between two strategies: a horizontal
      // cut (clear on the text side, full on the photo side — for the
      // wide desktop layout where text and photo sit side by side) and
      // a vertical cut (clear above, full below — for mobile's stacked
      // layout, where text sits ABOVE the photo instead of beside it;
      // a horizontal-only mask bled straight through the lede paragraph
      // there, since there's no "photo side" on a single-column layout).
      // Which one applies is read straight from the canvas's own aspect
      // ratio, so it tracks the CSS breakpoints without duplicating them
      // here.
      "  vec2 uv01=gl_FragCoord.xy/r;",
      "  float xMask=smoothstep(0.36,0.54,uv01.x);",
      "  float yMask=1.0-smoothstep(0.28,0.48,uv01.y);",
      "  float vert=smoothstep(1.0,0.6,r.x/r.y);",
      "  float mask=mix(xMask,yMask,vert);",
      "  shaded=mix(uBase,shaded,mask);",
      "  gl_FragColor=vec4(shaded,1.0);",
      "}"
    ].join("\n");

    function compile(type, src) {
      var s = gl.createShader(type);
      gl.shaderSource(s, src);
      gl.compileShader(s);
      return s;
    }
    var pr = gl.createProgram();
    gl.attachShader(pr, compile(gl.VERTEX_SHADER, vs));
    gl.attachShader(pr, compile(gl.FRAGMENT_SHADER, fs));
    gl.linkProgram(pr);
    gl.useProgram(pr);

    var buf = gl.createBuffer();
    gl.bindBuffer(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, buf);
    gl.bufferData(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, new Float32Array([-1, -1, 1, -1, -1, 1, 1, 1]), gl.STATIC_DRAW);
    var loc = gl.getAttribLocation(pr, "p");
    gl.enableVertexAttribArray(loc);
    gl.vertexAttribPointer(loc, 2, gl.FLOAT, false, 0, 0);

    var uT = gl.getUniformLocation(pr, "t");
    var uR = gl.getUniformLocation(pr, "r");
    var uOff = gl.getUniformLocation(pr, "uOff");
    var uZoom = gl.getUniformLocation(pr, "uZoom");
    var uBase = gl.getUniformLocation(pr, "uBase");
    var uNebBase = gl.getUniformLocation(pr, "uNebBase");
    var uNebAmp = gl.getUniformLocation(pr, "uNebAmp");

    // A single blue→cyan hue family (B amplified hardest, R barely at
    // all) instead of the source's literal red/magenta-dominant
    // amplitude — see the comment above for why. Same palette in both
    // themes; the `vivid` normalization in the shader is what makes it
    // read cleanly against either background, not a per-theme recolor.
    var DARK = { base: [0.031, 0.031, 0.039], nebBase: [0.05, 0.12, 0.35], nebAmp: [1.5, 3.0, 5.5] };
    var LIGHT = { base: [1.0, 1.0, 1.0], nebBase: [0.05, 0.12, 0.35], nebAmp: [1.5, 3.0, 5.5] };

    function isDark() {
      var explicit = document.documentElement.getAttribute("data-theme");
      if (explicit === "dark") return true;
      if (explicit === "light") return false;
      return window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches;
    }

    function resize() {
      var rect = cv.getBoundingClientRect();
      var dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, 2);
      var w = Math.max(1, Math.round(rect.width * dpr));
      var h = Math.max(1, Math.round(rect.height * dpr));
      if (cv.width !== w || cv.height !== h) {
        cv.width = w;
        cv.height = h;
        gl.viewport(0, 0, w, h);
        gl.uniform2f(uR, w, h);
        var minwh = Math.min(w, h);
        gl.uniform2f(uOff, 1.0 * (w / minwh), (1 - 0.48) * (h / minwh));
        gl.uniform1f(uZoom, 0.5);
      }
    }
    window.addEventListener("resize", resize);
    resize();

    var reduceMotion = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
    var t0 = performance.now();

    function draw() {
      var theme = isDark() ? DARK : LIGHT;
      gl.uniform3f(uBase, theme.base[0], theme.base[1], theme.base[2]);
      gl.uniform3f(uNebBase, theme.nebBase[0], theme.nebBase[1], theme.nebBase[2]);
      gl.uniform3f(uNebAmp, theme.nebAmp[0], theme.nebAmp[1], theme.nebAmp[2]);
      gl.uniform1f(uT, (performance.now() - t0) / 1000);
      gl.drawArrays(gl.TRIANGLE_STRIP, 0, 4);
      if (!reduceMotion) requestAnimationFrame(draw);
    }
    draw();
  })();

  // Hero photo fade-in — tied to the image's actual load event rather than
  // a fixed delay (see the html.js .heroS__img rule in _home.scss for why:
  // a timed reveal would run out regardless of whether the image had
  // actually arrived yet, which is the "doesn't fade in smoothly" bug this
  // fixes). Not using img[loading="lazy"] (the sitewide handler in ds.njk)
  // because this photo is eager — it's above the fold.
  (function () {
    var img = document.querySelector(".heroS__img");
    if (!img) return;
    if (img.complete && img.naturalWidth > 0) {
      img.classList.add("is-loaded");
      return;
    }
    img.addEventListener("load", function () { img.classList.add("is-loaded"); });
    img.addEventListener("error", function () { img.classList.add("is-loaded"); });
  })();
</script>

{# -------------------------------------------------------- Latest post -- #}
{% if latest %}
<div class="latestBand">
  <a class="latest" href="{{ latest.url }}">
    {% if latest.data.image %}
      <img class="latest__thumb" src="{{ latest.data.image }}" alt="" loading="lazy">
    {% endif %}
    <span class="latest__body">
      <span class="latest__kicker">Latest from the blog <span class="latest__dot">·</span> {{ latest.date | dateReadable }}</span>
      <span class="latest__t">{{ latest.data.title }}</span>
    </span>
    <span class="latest__cta">Read the post <i class="icon-arrow-right" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
  </a>
</div>
{% endif %}

{# ------------------------------------------------------ Worlds splash -- #}
<div data-theme="dark">
  <section class="wsplash" data-reveal>
    <div class="wsplash__in">
      <p class="wsplash__eyebrow">{{ home.worlds.eyebrow }}</p>
      <img class="wsplash__mark" src="{{ home.worlds.mark }}" alt="Worlds">
      <h2 class="wsplash__h">{{ home.worlds.title }}</h2>
      <p class="wsplash__lede">{{ home.worlds.description }}</p>
      {% if home.worlds.description2 %}<p class="wsplash__lede">{{ home.worlds.description2 }}</p>{% endif %}
      <div class="wsplash__actions">
        {{ button("See the work", home.worlds.href, "inverse", "md", "rounded", "", "arrow-right") }}
        <a class="wsplash__link" href="{{ home.worlds.secondaryUrl }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          {{ home.worlds.secondaryLabel }} <i class="icon-arrow-up-right-from-square" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
    {% if home.worlds.video %}
      <div class="wsplash__shot">
        <video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="auto" poster="{{ home.worlds.image }}">
          <source src="{{ home.worlds.video }}" type="video/mp4">
        </video>
        {{ muteToggle() }}
      </div>
    {% elif home.worlds.image %}
      <div class="wsplash__shot">
        <img src="{{ home.worlds.image }}" alt="{{ home.worlds.imageAlt }}" loading="lazy">
      </div>
    {% endif %}
  </section>
  <div class="specband">
    <div class="specband__in">
      {% for spec in home.worlds.specs %}
        <div>
          <p class="specband__k">{{ spec.k }}</p>
          <p class="specband__v">{{ spec.v }}</p>
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

{# ----------------------------------------------------- Selected work -- #}
<div id="work">
  <div class="sectionTop">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">What else I've built</p>
      <h2>Selected work &amp; impact.</h2>
    </div>
    {# Hidden for now — re-enable by uncommenting if we bring the portfolio download back.
    {% for download in site.downloads %}
      {% if download.title == "portfolio" %}
        {{ button("Download my portfolio", download.url, "secondary", "md", "rounded", "download", "", true) }}
      {% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
    #}
  </div>

  <div class="showcase-stack">
    {% for company in home.major %}
      {{ showcaseBanner(company) }}
      <p class="quoteline">&ldquo;{{ company.quote }}&rdquo;</p>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

  <div class="chaptersTop">
    <p class="eyebrow">Earlier chapters</p>
  </div>
  <div class="chapters">
    {% for item in home.earlierChapters %}{{ showcaseBanner(item, "compact") }}{% endfor %}
  </div>

</div>

{# --------------------------------------------------------- Statement -- #}
<section class="speak" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="speak__h">{{ home.statement.title }}</h2>
  <p class="speak__p">{{ home.statement.body }}</p>
  <div class="speak__strip">
    {% for shot in home.statement.strip %}
      <img src="{{ shot.src }}" alt="{{ shot.alt }}" loading="lazy">
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
  <a class="speak__link" href="{{ home.statement.linkUrl }}">
    {{ home.statement.linkLabel }} <i class="icon-arrow-right" aria-hidden="true"></i>
  </a>
</section>

{# ---------------------------------------------- Approach + experience -- #}
<div class="twoCol" data-reveal>
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">My approach</p>
    <h2>Leadership that ships.</h2>
    <p>{{ home.approach }}</p>
    <a class="inline-link" href="/resume/">See all experience <i class="icon-arrow-right" aria-hidden="true"></i></a>
  </div>
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom: 24px;">Work experience</p>
    <div class="twoCol__roles">
      {% for role in home.work %}{{ workItem(role) }}{% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

{# -------------------------------------------------------------- Blog -- #}
<div>
  <div class="sectionTop" style="padding-top: 0;">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">From the blog</p>
      <h2>What I'm thinking about.</h2>
    </div>
    <a class="inline-link" href="/blog/">Go to blog <i class="icon-arrow-right" aria-hidden="true"></i></a>
  </div>
  <div class="bloggrid" data-reveal>
    {% for post in collections.articles | limit(3) %}
      {{ articleCard({
        title: post.data.title,
        url: post.url,
        date: post.date | dateReadable,
        summary: post.data.summary,
        image: post.data.image,
        tags: post.data.tags
      }, "grid") }}
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
</div>
